<script data-rh="true" id="seo-schema-0" type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"How to Sell Your Music Rights in 2026: The Complete Guide","description":"Learn how to sell your music rights in 2026. This comprehensive guide covers catalog valuation (3–15x multiples), the step-by-step sale process, buyer types, tax implications, and strategies to maximize your payout. Built for independent creators by SPACE.","url":"https://spacemusic.io/guides/how-to-sell-music-rights/","publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"SPACE","url":"https://spacemusic.io"}}</script>
<script data-rh="true" id="seo-schema-1" type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does it take to sell a music catalog?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The timeline depends on the sale channel and the complexity of your catalog. Direct buyer transactions typically close in 2 to 8 weeks from first contact, including due diligence and paperwork. Auction marketplaces run 4 to 12 weeks because of the listing period and bidding process. Catalogs with co-ownership issues, uncleared samples, or incomplete documentation can take longer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sell my music rights if I'm signed to a distributor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but your distribution agreement matters. Most modern distribution deals are non-exclusive licenses, meaning you retain ownership of your masters and can sell them. However, some agreements include exclusivity windows or transfer restrictions. Review your distributor contract before initiating a sale, and factor in any notice period required for transitioning to the buyer's preferred distributor."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Net Publisher's Share (NPS) and why does it matter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"NPS is the royalty revenue that actually reaches you after all intermediary fees — platform commissions, distributor cuts, PRO administrative costs — are deducted. Buyers base their valuation multiples on NPS, not gross revenue, because NPS represents the true economic benefit of owning the catalog. If your gross streaming revenue is $15,000 but your distributor takes 15%, your NPS is approximately $12,750."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a lawyer to sell my music catalog?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It's not legally required, but it's strongly recommended. A music attorney will review the purchase agreement to ensure the terms protect your interests, flag any problematic clauses, and advise on deal structure. The cost — typically $500 to $2,000 for a contract review — is trivial relative to the deal value and can prevent costly mistakes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sell just my master rights and keep my publishing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Absolutely. Masters and publishing are separate copyrights that can be sold independently. Some creators sell their masters while retaining publishing to continue collecting songwriter royalties. This is common when the creator wants to maintain their connection to the compositional side of the work. However, selling both together typically yields a higher total valuation because the buyer controls all income streams."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will selling my catalog affect my ability to release new music?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A catalog sale transfers the rights to existing recordings and/or compositions. It does not affect your ability to write, record, or release new music going forward. Your future works are separate assets that you own from the moment of creation. Some purchase agreements include a right of first refusal on future releases, but this is negotiable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know if I'm getting a fair price for my catalog?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The best way to verify fair pricing is to solicit multiple offers. If three independent buyers each value your catalog at 8 to 10x NPS, you can be confident the market rate is in that range. You should also compare your multiple against published benchmarks: independent catalogs typically trade at 3 to 15x depending on genre, revenue trajectory, and metadata quality. A free valuation from platforms like SPACE can give you a data-driven starting point."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens to my royalties after I sell?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Once the sale closes and the rights transfer is complete, future royalties flow to the new owner. There is typically a transition period of 30 to 90 days during which some payments may still be directed to you due to reporting delays from PROs and distributors. Your purchase agreement should specify how these transitional payments are handled — usually the seller remits any post-closing royalties to the buyer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I buy back my music rights after selling them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no automatic right to repurchase, but it's possible if the buyer is willing. Some creators negotiate a buyback clause in the original purchase agreement, specifying conditions and pricing for repurchase. Without such a clause, you would need to negotiate a new acquisition from the current rights holder at whatever the market price is at that time — which could be higher or lower than what you originally received."}}]}</script>
<script data-rh="true" id="seo-schema-2" type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://spacemusic.io/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Guides","item":"https://spacemusic.io/guides/how-to-sell-music-rights/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"How to Sell Your Music Rights in 2026: The Complete Guide","item":"https://spacemusic.io/guides/how-to-sell-music-rights/"}]}</script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/journey-cta-632796e93c26.css"></head>

